{"kind":"assert","text":"Rulings on 3bf7e5aa, 5e30241b, e4b72290, 0a5f97ba and a4a587c9 for\nthe cutover head (05d618e2).\n\n3bf7e5aa and 5e30241b, copies that reach the judge. Choose (b).\nEvery checked copy enters `facts`, as on main: a public copy after\nthe birth check, and a register or membership copy after the\n`#privateCopy` context check. `#prove` still proves every fact by\nreplay or by anchor. The cutover removed that reach with no\nruling, so (b) restores it. Add one test: a copy that fails its\ncheck does not reach the judge. Q-V2 follows this change. Tell me\nif 18.46 or 18.52 still reads `incomplete`.\n\ne4b72290 question 1. Yes. `growArchivePolicies` and the config\ncheck in `apps/room/archive.ts` take the trusted release, the\npublisher and the entry as parameters. `worker.grow.ts` passes the\nthree constants of today, so production does not change in this\nhead. After I sign the new release, my configuration head\nreplaces those constants.\n\ne4b72290 question 2, the \"old R17 Room A shape\" test. Its\nacceptance case is that the archive advances from a published\nprefix of many passes. Move the test to the source-built demo if\nyou can build that history within the test's present time. If\nyou cannot, retire it with a note row, and name in that row the\nremaining test that covers an advance from a published prefix. If\nno test remains for that case, tell me before you retire it.\n\n0a5f97ba, the demo Site. Choose (a). Repair `demoSite` in this\nhead: its `room-summary` receiver pins `demoRoom`. The receiver\nrefuses the Room that the Site creates, so this is a defect, and\nmy release needs the repair. Report the new `demoSite` digest and\nevery other digest that changes before you commit the pin. I\nconfirm them in one act. The four scenarios then keep their\nacceptance case with a demo built from source.\n\na4a587c9, births in the per-entry retained-byte bound. Choose\n(b), with a stop. 4f697a7a item 5 says that the retained-byte caps\ncharge the birth bytes, and a bound that omits them promises less\nthan the scope keeps. This bound must be right at the cutover,\nbecause a stricter bound later would refuse definitions that\nrooms already hold.\n1. `fieldRetainedBytes` adds the birth term. Use the smallest\n   true bound on one birth. If genesis bytes have a bound below\n   `entryBytes`, use that bound and name it.\n2. Run the capacity checks over every pinned definition. If one\n   fails, stop and report the definition, the bound and the need,\n   and change no cap. I rule then.\n3. State in the note what a scope does at runtime when its\n   retained bytes pass the reserved capacity.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}